protocol='''Cross-scale evaluation of a weather-driven Septoria model in European winter wheat

Primary research question

Predictive skill across independent seasons and locations, and its transfer to wheat-growing regions, constitute the core study. Climate-change impacts are a conditional extension. Disease data volume alone does not establish primary–secondary process identifiability or geographical validity.

Model evaluation

Component tests distinguish host phenology, establishment and latent development, visible damage, reproductive development and transmission. Full infection-force evaluation requires external inoculum or defensible process constraints and a calibrated observation model. Same-rank disease series require plot-level clustering. Forward-year and country or spatial-block holdouts compare predictions against persistence, epidemic trend and mechanism ablations. Disease-onset errors, severity RMSE/MAE, seasonal burden and calibrated interval coverage provide complementary outcomes where their observations exist. Primary onset and airborne inoculum are currently unobserved in most BASF trials.

Current evidence

The initial conditional secondary-only SEIR yields forward-year RMSE34.58 and32.43 percentage points for2018 and2019; the global logit trend yields24.70 and20.10. Releasing the transmission bound improves some errors but does not remove that gap. Twenty-five sequences begin with zero disease, requiring a hidden, external or cross-leaf infection source. German T–P–V test MAE8.023days supports its original station-transfer domain, with no independent disease validation. These findings support revision of the compartment observation mapping and canopy inoculum terms before continental disease application.

European application

A wheat-specific harvested-area layer, regional sowing calendar, cultivar or resistance scenarios, daily weather and leaf-layer transport define the regional simulation domain. Supported outcomes are spatial distributions of infection timing, stage-specific risk, epidemic burden and uncertainty. Wheat-area weighting is required for regional burden; WorldCereal winter cereals are a host-context layer rather than a wheat mask. Environmental extrapolation maps identify regions without validation support. Europe-region ERA5 exposure rasters and a verified hourly extraction path are available; validated continental disease maps are not yet generated.

Conditional climate extension

Climate scenarios address a remaining question about direct pathogen-weather effects, altered host phenology or regional shifts after model evaluation. Bias-adjusted multi-model ensembles and baseline/future periods retain forcing uncertainty. Fixed-host and responsive-host comparisons separate phenological mediation; inoculum, resistance and sowing scenarios expose management assumptions. Historical ERA5 and simple temperature perturbations do not constitute future climate projections. Climate extension is not a substitute for predictive skill.
'''
(OUT/'European_study_protocol.txt').write_text(protocol)
print('Methods.docx, Methods.txt and European_study_protocol.txt generated.')
